WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:06.150
Process and control telemetry describes the physical operation and the control system's view of it.

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:10.587
Sources may include sensor values, actuator states, alarms, setpoints, controller modes,

00:00:10.587 --> 00:00:15.023
equipment status, and process trends retained by HMIs, controllers, SCADA platforms,

00:00:15.023 --> 00:00:15.830
or historians.

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:20.670
Sensor values show measured conditions such as pressure, temperature, flow, level, position,

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:21.477
or speed.

00:00:21.477 --> 00:00:25.510
Actuator states indicate whether equipment is running, stopped, open, closed,

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:27.527
or in another commanded state.

00:00:27.527 --> 00:00:31.963
Setpoints describe intended targets, while controller status can reveal mode, health,

00:00:31.963 --> 00:00:33.577
communications, or fault conditions.

00:00:33.577 --> 00:00:37.610
Alarms call attention to conditions defined by the control design.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:41.240
Their priority, acknowledgment, suppression, and duration provide important context.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:46.887
A single alarm code is rarely enough; analysts need to know the associated equipment,

00:00:46.887 --> 00:00:48.903
process state, and operating procedure.

00:00:48.903 --> 00:00:51.727
Trends show how values change over time.

00:00:51.727 --> 00:00:56.970
They can help investigators compare a network or identity event with process behavior.

00:00:56.970 --> 00:01:01.407
A changed setpoint followed by a process deviation may be relevant,

00:01:01.407 --> 00:01:04.230
but correlation alone does not establish cause.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:07.860
Equipment failures, calibration problems, maintenance, process transitions, operator actions,

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:10.683
and cyber events can produce similar observations.

00:01:10.683 --> 00:01:14.717
Units, tag identity, sampling rate, clock accuracy, data quality flags,

00:01:14.717 --> 00:01:17.137
and historian gaps all affect interpretation.

00:01:17.137 --> 00:01:21.170
Protect process telemetry because its integrity and availability influence decisions.

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:25.607
Limit access, monitor configuration and tag changes, and preserve trustworthy retention.

00:01:25.607 --> 00:01:29.640
Security teams should use this evidence with operators and engineers,

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:33.673
who can explain whether the observed behavior is expected, unsafe,

00:01:33.673 --> 00:01:36.900
or simply a normal response to changing conditions.
